window.CHDATA=window.CHDATA||{};window.CHDATA[19]={"num": 19, "title": "Invasive Fungal Infections: Invasive Pulmonary Aspergillosis and Invasive Candidiasis", "keyPoints": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA)</strong> is caused by an environmental <strong>mould</strong> (mostly <strong><em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em></strong>) acquired by <strong>inhaling conidia</strong>. <strong>Invasive candidiasis (IC)</strong> is caused by <strong>yeasts</strong> from the patient's <strong>own gut or skin flora</strong> that reach the bloodstream.</li>\n<li>The classic IPA host has <strong>prolonged, profound neutropenia</strong> or an <strong>allogeneic stem cell transplant (HSCT)</strong>, but IPA is increasingly seen in <strong>ICU patients</strong> (COPD on steroids, cirrhosis, <strong>severe influenza</strong>, <strong>COVID-19</strong>). IC is a disease of the <strong>ICU and surgical ward</strong>: <strong>central venous catheter</strong>, <strong>broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong>, <strong>parenteral nutrition</strong>, <strong>abdominal surgery</strong>.</li>\n<li>IPA diagnosis rests on <strong>host factor + chest CT + mycology</strong> (<strong>galactomannan</strong>, <em>Aspergillus</em> PCR, culture), classified as <strong>proven / probable / possible</strong> (EORTC/MSGERC 2020). In neutropenic patients the typical early CT sign is a <strong>nodule with a halo sign</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood cultures detect only about half of IC</strong>; <strong>β-D-glucan</strong> and <strong>T2Candida</strong> add sensitivity, and β-D-glucan is most useful for its <strong>negative predictive value</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>First-line IPA therapy: a mould-active triazole</strong> (<strong>voriconazole</strong>, <strong>isavuconazole</strong> or <strong>posaconazole</strong>), with <strong>therapeutic drug monitoring</strong> for voriconazole and posaconazole. <strong>First-line candidemia therapy: an echinocandin</strong>, with step-down to <strong>fluconazole</strong> when the isolate is susceptible and the patient is stable.</li>\n<li>Every candidemia needs <strong>removal of the central venous catheter</strong> (when it is the likely source), <strong>repeat blood cultures</strong> until negative, a <strong>dilated fundoscopy</strong>, and <strong>14 days of therapy after the first negative blood culture</strong> (uncomplicated cases).</li>\n<li><strong>Antifungal resistance is rising:</strong> azole-resistant <em>A. fumigatus</em> (environmental <strong>TR34/L98H</strong>), and multidrug-resistant <strong><em>Candida auris</em></strong> (now <em>Candidozyma auris</em>), a hospital outbreak pathogen.</li>\n</ul>", "redFlags": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neutropenic fever persisting despite broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> = think <strong>IPA</strong> → <strong>chest CT</strong>, galactomannan, BAL.</li>\n<li><strong>Halo sign early, air crescent late</strong> (at neutrophil recovery); a <strong>reversed halo</strong> sign suggests <strong>mucormycosis</strong>, against which <strong>voriconazole is inactive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serum galactomannan is less sensitive in non-neutropenic patients and on mould-active prophylaxis</strong>: send <strong>BAL galactomannan</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>β-D-glucan is pan-fungal</strong>: useful to <strong>rule out</strong> IC, not to name the fungus.</li>\n<li><strong>ICU patients with severe influenza or COVID-19</strong> can develop IPA without classic host factors.</li>\n<li><strong>IPA: voriconazole/isavuconazole/posaconazole first-line</strong>; <strong>fluconazole has no activity against <em>Aspergillus</em></strong>; check voriconazole levels (TDM).</li>\n<li><strong>Enlarging nodules in the first 7–10 days</strong> of therapy are <strong>not failure</strong> by themselves.</li>\n<li><strong>Candidemia: echinocandin first</strong>, step down to fluconazole after <strong>5–7 days</strong> if stable, susceptible, cultures negative.</li>\n<li><strong>Never ignore a positive blood culture for <em>Candida</em></strong>: it is <strong>never a contaminant</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Candidemia checklist:</strong> <strong>remove the CVC</strong>, <strong>repeat blood cultures</strong>, <strong>fundoscopy</strong>, consider <strong>echocardiography</strong>, <strong>14 days from the first negative culture</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Echinocandins penetrate eye and urine poorly</strong>: use <strong>fluconazole</strong> for endophthalmitis and <em>Candida</em> cystitis.</li>\n<li><strong><em>C. krusei</em> is intrinsically fluconazole-resistant</strong>; <strong><em>C. glabrata</em> has reduced fluconazole susceptibility</strong>; <strong><em>C. auris</em> is multidrug-resistant</strong> and spreads in hospitals.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Candida</em> in sputum or BAL, or asymptomatic candiduria, usually means colonization</strong>: do not treat.</li>\n</ul>", "intro": "", "sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Microbiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "1.1", "title": "<em>Aspergillus</em>", "html": "<ul>\n<li><em>Aspergillus</em> is a ubiquitous <strong>filamentous fungus (mould)</strong> found in <strong>soil, decaying vegetation, dust and building construction sites</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li>It reproduces asexually by producing <strong>conidia (spores) 2–3 µm in diameter</strong>, small enough to reach the <strong>alveoli</strong> when inhaled<sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li>In tissue it grows as <strong>narrow (3–6 µm), regularly septate hyphae</strong> with <strong>acute-angle (about 45°) dichotomous branching</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>A. fumigatus</em></strong> grows well at <strong>37 °C and above</strong> (thermotolerance), which partly explains why it is the most common human pathogen<sup class=\"cite\">[28]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Species</th>\n<th>Clinical relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>A. fumigatus</em></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Most common cause</strong> of invasive aspergillosis; <strong>azole resistance</strong> emerging (section 6.1)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,20]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>A. flavus</em></strong></td>\n<td>Second most common in some regions; <strong>sinusitis</strong>, cutaneous and post-surgical infections<sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>A. terreus</em></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Intrinsically resistant to amphotericin B</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>A. niger</em></strong></td>\n<td>Mostly colonization, otitis externa; uncommon cause of invasive disease<sup class=\"cite\">[28]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cryptic species</strong> (e.g. <em>A. lentulus</em>, <em>A. calidoustus</em>)</td>\n<td>Look like <em>A. fumigatus</em> or other species under the microscope but are often <strong>resistant to azoles</strong>; identification needs molecular methods or MALDI-TOF<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spectrum of disease caused by <em>Aspergillus</em>:</strong> the same organism causes very different diseases depending on the host's immunity<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>. This chapter covers <strong>IPA</strong> only.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Host</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA)</strong></td>\n<td>Asthma, cystic fibrosis</td>\n<td><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to <em>Aspergillus</em> antigens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aspergilloma / chronic pulmonary aspergillosis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pre-existing cavity</strong> (old TB, sarcoidosis, COPD)</td>\n<td>Colonization or slowly invasive infection of a cavity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Immunocompromised</strong> or critically ill</td>\n<td><strong>Tissue and vascular invasion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Differential at the microscope (high yield):</strong> <strong>Mucorales</strong> (agents of mucormycosis) produce <strong>broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae</strong> with <strong>right-angle branching</strong>; this matters because <strong>voriconazole has no activity against Mucorales</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "1.2", "title": "<em>Candida</em>", "html": "<ul>\n<li><em>Candida</em> species are <strong>yeasts</strong> (round to oval budding cells, 4–6 µm) that are part of the <strong>normal flora</strong> of the <strong>gastrointestinal tract, mouth, vagina and skin</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>C. albicans</em></strong> forms <strong>true hyphae</strong> (positive <strong>germ tube test</strong>) and <strong>pseudohyphae</strong>; <strong><em>C. glabrata</em> does not form pseudohyphae</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>New names (taxonomy revised; the old names are still used in practice)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>: <em>C. glabrata</em> → <strong><em>Nakaseomyces glabratus</em></strong>; <em>C. krusei</em> → <strong><em>Pichia kudriavzevii</em></strong>; <em>C. auris</em> → <strong><em>Candidozyma auris</em></strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Species</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. albicans</em></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Most common</strong> species; usually <strong>fluconazole-susceptible</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. glabrata</em></strong> (<em>N. glabratus</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Reduced susceptibility to fluconazole</strong>; more common in <strong>older patients</strong> and after azole exposure; <strong>echinocandin resistance</strong> (<em>FKS</em> gene mutations) is emerging<sup class=\"cite\">[4,6,24]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. parapsilosis</em></strong></td>\n<td>Lives on the <strong>skin and hands</strong>; linked to <strong>central venous catheters</strong>, <strong>parenteral nutrition</strong> and <strong>neonates</strong>; forms <strong>biofilms</strong>; <strong>higher echinocandin MICs</strong>; <strong>fluconazole-resistant clones</strong> are spreading<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5,24]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. tropicalis</em></strong></td>\n<td>Associated with <strong>neutropenia</strong> and hematologic malignancy<sup class=\"cite\">[4,6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. krusei</em></strong> (<em>P. kudriavzevii</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Intrinsically resistant to fluconazole</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,26]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. auris</em></strong> (<em>Candidozyma auris</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Multidrug-resistant</strong> (most isolates fluconazole-resistant; some resistant to amphotericin B or echinocandins); <strong>persists on surfaces</strong> and colonizes skin; causes <strong>hospital outbreaks</strong>; often <strong>misidentified</strong> by conventional biochemical methods (MALDI-TOF or sequencing needed)<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In its <strong>2022 Fungal Priority Pathogens List</strong>, the <strong>WHO</strong> placed <strong><em>Candida auris</em></strong>, <strong><em>Candida albicans</em></strong> and <strong><em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em></strong> (with <em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em>) in the <strong>critical priority group</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "2", "title": "Epidemiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "2.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<ul>\n<li>IPA occurs in hosts with <strong>defects in neutrophil or macrophage function</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk group</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Classic hematologic hosts</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Prolonged profound neutropenia</strong> (&lt;0.5 × 10⁹/L for <strong>&gt;10 days</strong>), <strong>acute myeloid leukemia (AML)/myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS)</strong> under induction chemotherapy, <strong>allogeneic HSCT</strong>, especially with <strong>graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunosuppressive drugs</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Prolonged high-dose corticosteroids</strong>, T-cell immunosuppressants, <strong>Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitors</strong> (e.g. ibrutinib)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Solid organ transplant</strong></td>\n<td>Highest risk in <strong>lung transplant</strong> recipients (also tracheobronchitis at the anastomosis)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inherited immunodeficiency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ICU patients without classic host factors</strong></td>\n<td><strong>COPD on corticosteroids</strong>, <strong>decompensated liver cirrhosis</strong>, solid tumors, <strong>severe influenza</strong> (influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis, <strong>IAPA</strong>) and <strong>COVID-19</strong> (COVID-19-associated pulmonary aspergillosis, <strong>CAPA</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[17,18,19,27]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mortality is high.</strong> In the trial that established voriconazole, <strong>12-week survival was about 71% with voriconazole versus 58% with amphotericin B deoxycholate</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>; all-cause mortality at <strong>day 42</strong> was about <strong>15–20%</strong> in later first-line trials<sup class=\"cite\">[10,11]</sup>. Outcomes in ICU patients are worse<sup class=\"cite\">[17,18,27]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Azole-resistant <em>A. fumigatus</em></strong> is reported worldwide. Most resistant isolates carry the <strong><em>cyp51A</em> TR34/L98H</strong> mutation, selected in the <strong>environment by agricultural azole fungicides</strong>, so resistance can be found in <strong>azole-naïve patients</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2,20]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Invasive candidiasis</strong> includes <strong>candidemia</strong> (bloodstream infection) and <strong>deep-seated candidiasis</strong> (e.g. intra-abdominal abscess or peritonitis), which may occur <strong>with or without candidemia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Candida</em> is among the most common causes of hospital-acquired bloodstream infection</strong> (fourth in a large US surveillance study)<sup class=\"cite\">[29]</sup>. <strong>Crude mortality of candidemia remains high</strong>, commonly reported around <strong>30–40%</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Species distribution is shifting toward non-<em>albicans</em> species</strong> (<em>C. glabrata</em> in Northern Europe and the US; <em>C. parapsilosis</em> in Southern Europe and Latin America), driven partly by <strong>azole exposure</strong> and local practice<sup class=\"cite\">[24]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Major risk factors for invasive candidiasis<sup class=\"cite\">[4,6,7]</sup></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Central venous catheter (CVC)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> (suppress the bacterial flora and favor <em>Candida</em> overgrowth)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total parenteral nutrition (TPN)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Abdominal surgery</strong>, especially <strong>recurrent gastrointestinal perforation</strong> or <strong>anastomotic leak</strong>; <strong>acute necrotizing pancreatitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prolonged ICU stay</strong>, mechanical ventilation, <strong>renal replacement therapy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Candida</em> colonization at multiple sites</strong> (multifocal colonization)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neutropenia</strong> and <strong>chemotherapy-induced mucositis</strong>; <strong>allogeneic HSCT</strong>; <strong>solid organ transplant</strong> (liver, pancreas, small bowel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prematurity / very low birth weight</strong>; <strong>injection drug use</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Candida score</strong> (León 2006) helps decide whom to treat empirically in the ICU: <strong>TPN (1 point)</strong>, <strong>surgery (1)</strong>, <strong>multifocal colonization (1)</strong>, <strong>severe sepsis (2)</strong>. A score <strong>&gt;2.5</strong> identifies patients at higher risk; a low score has a <strong>high negative predictive value</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[21]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "3", "title": "Pathogenesis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Entry:</strong> <strong>inhaled conidia</strong> reach the alveoli (or deposit in the <strong>paranasal sinuses</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Defense in two lines</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[28]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>alveolar macrophages</strong> ingest and kill <strong>conidia</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>neutrophils</strong> kill <strong>hyphae</strong> that escape the macrophages.</li>\n<li><strong>When neutrophils are missing (neutropenia):</strong> conidia germinate into hyphae that <strong>invade blood vessels (angioinvasion)</strong>, causing <strong>thrombosis, hemorrhagic infarction and necrosis</strong> of lung tissue. This is the basis of the <strong>halo sign</strong> (a nodule = infarct, surrounded by ground-glass = hemorrhage) on CT (section 5.1)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>When neutrophils return</strong> (marrow recovery), the necrotic center separates from the surrounding lung, producing the <strong>air crescent sign</strong> and cavitation; sudden <strong>hemoptysis</strong> can occur at this stage<sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>In non-neutropenic hosts (corticosteroids, GVHD, ICU):</strong> macrophage and neutrophil function is impaired but cells are present. Disease is <strong>less angioinvasive</strong> and more <strong>airway-invasive and inflammatory</strong>, so <strong>serum galactomannan is less often positive</strong> (section 5.1)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Viral pneumonia (influenza, COVID-19)</strong> damages the <strong>airway epithelium</strong> and impairs local immunity, allowing <em>Aspergillus</em> to invade the airways (<strong>tracheobronchitis</strong>) and lung<sup class=\"cite\">[17,18]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dissemination:</strong> by the bloodstream to the <strong>brain</strong>, skin and other organs<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Two routes into the bloodstream</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>endogenous, from the gut:</strong> <em>Candida</em> overgrows after antibiotics and crosses a <strong>damaged intestinal mucosa</strong> (surgery, chemotherapy mucositis);</li>\n<li><strong>exogenous, from the skin:</strong> <em>Candida</em> (typically <strong><em>C. parapsilosis</em></strong>, often via <strong>healthcare workers' hands</strong>) colonizes a <strong>CVC</strong> and forms a <strong>biofilm</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Virulence factors</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>: <strong>adhesins</strong>, the switch from <strong>yeast to hyphal form</strong> (tissue invasion), <strong>secreted aspartyl proteases</strong> and the peptide toxin <strong>candidalysin</strong>, and <strong>biofilm</strong> formation (biofilm-embedded yeasts are poorly reached by azoles; <strong>echinocandins and lipid amphotericin B</strong> are more active against biofilms).</li>\n<li><strong>Host defense</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>: <strong>neutrophils</strong> are the key defense against invasive disease. <strong>Th17/IL-17 immunity</strong> protects mucosal surfaces, which is why <strong>mucocutaneous candidiasis</strong> (thrush) is typical of <strong>T-cell defects</strong> (e.g. HIV) rather than of neutropenia.</li>\n<li><strong>Hematogenous seeding</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,6]</sup>: <strong>eye</strong> (chorioretinitis, endophthalmitis), <strong>kidney</strong>, <strong>liver and spleen</strong>, <strong>heart valves</strong>, <strong>bones and joints</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "4", "title": "Signs and Symptoms", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "4.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>The cardinal presentation:</strong> a <strong>neutropenic patient with fever that persists despite broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory symptoms:</strong> <strong>cough</strong>, <strong>dyspnea</strong>, <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong> (pulmonary infarction) and <strong>hemoptysis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids blunt fever;</strong> in ICU patients the main clue is <strong>worsening respiratory function despite appropriate antibacterial therapy</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17,18,27]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Other forms</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong><em>Aspergillus</em> tracheobronchitis</strong> (lung transplant, influenza): ulcers, plaques or <strong>pseudomembranes</strong> at bronchoscopy;</li>\n<li><strong>invasive sinusitis</strong>: facial pain, nasal discharge, <strong>black necrotic lesions</strong> of the nasal mucosa or palate;</li>\n<li><strong>cerebral aspergillosis</strong>: <strong>focal deficits, seizures</strong>, altered mental status.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Candidemia</strong> usually presents as <strong>fever</strong> or <strong>sepsis/septic shock that does not respond to antibacterial therapy</strong>; it is <strong>clinically indistinguishable from bacterial sepsis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clinical form</th>\n<th>Clues</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ocular candidiasis</strong> (chorioretinitis, endophthalmitis)</td>\n<td>Often <strong>asymptomatic</strong> in early stages; later <strong>blurred vision, floaters</strong>; <strong>white fluffy retinal lesions</strong> extending into the vitreous<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intra-abdominal candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Peritonitis or abscess</strong> after abdominal surgery, perforation or anastomotic leak<sup class=\"cite\">[4,7]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic disseminated (hepatosplenic) candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td>Hematologic patient with <strong>fever that appears or persists when neutrophils recover</strong>, raised <strong>alkaline phosphatase</strong>, and <strong>\"bull's-eye\" lesions</strong> in liver and spleen<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Candida</em> endocarditis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Prosthetic valves</strong>, injection drug use; <strong>large vegetations</strong> and embolic events<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Osteoarticular candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td>Vertebral osteomyelitis/spondylodiscitis, arthritis, sometimes weeks to months after candidemia<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Skin lesions</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Macronodular</strong> lesions in neutropenic patients<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Colonization is not infection</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong><em>Candida</em> in respiratory secretions</strong> almost never means <em>Candida</em> pneumonia and <strong>should not be treated</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>asymptomatic candiduria</strong> is usually colonization (often catheter-related) and generally <strong>should not be treated</strong> (exceptions in section 6.2).</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "5", "title": "Diagnosis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "5.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<p><strong>When to suspect:</strong> neutropenic fever that persists <strong>&gt;3–5 days</strong> despite antibiotics; a <strong>new nodule or infiltrate</strong> in a high-risk host; an ICU patient with influenza, COVID-19, COPD on steroids or cirrhosis whose <strong>respiratory status worsens despite antibiotics</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,17,18,27]</sup>.</p>\n<p><strong>EORTC/MSGERC 2020 definitions (immunocompromised patients)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Requirements</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Proven</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Histopathology</strong> showing hyphae with <strong>tissue damage</strong>, or <strong>culture</strong> of <em>Aspergillus</em> from a <strong>normally sterile site</strong> (biopsy, needle aspirate)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Probable</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Host factor + clinical (radiological) feature + mycological evidence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Possible</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Host factor + clinical feature</strong>, <strong>without</strong> mycological evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Examples<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Host factors</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Recent neutropenia</strong> (&lt;0.5 × 10⁹/L for &gt;10 days), <strong>hematologic malignancy</strong>, <strong>allogeneic HSCT</strong>, <strong>solid organ transplant</strong>, <strong>prolonged corticosteroids</strong> (≥0.3 mg/kg/day prednisone equivalent for ≥3 weeks in the past 60 days), <strong>T-cell or B-cell immunosuppressants</strong> (e.g. BTK inhibitors), <strong>inherited immunodeficiency</strong> (e.g. CGD), <strong>acute GVHD grade III–IV</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Clinical features (CT)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Dense, well-circumscribed lesion(s) with or without a halo sign</strong>, <strong>air crescent sign</strong>, <strong>cavity</strong>, <strong>wedge-shaped, segmental or lobar consolidation</strong>; or tracheobronchial ulceration, nodule or pseudomembrane at bronchoscopy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mycological evidence</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Aspergillus</em> galactomannan</strong> in serum/plasma, <strong>BAL</strong> or CSF above set thresholds; <strong><em>Aspergillus</em> PCR</strong> (e.g. two positive blood samples, or BAL); <strong>culture or microscopy</strong> of <em>Aspergillus</em> from sputum, BAL or bronchial brush</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chest CT (not chest X-ray)</strong> is the imaging test of choice; the X-ray is often normal early on<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CT sign</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Halo sign</strong></td>\n<td>Nodule surrounded by ground-glass opacity (hemorrhage around an infarct); <strong>early</strong> sign, typical of <strong>neutropenic</strong> patients<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Air crescent sign</strong></td>\n<td>Air separating necrotic tissue from surrounding lung; <strong>late</strong> sign, at <strong>neutrophil recovery</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reversed halo sign</strong></td>\n<td>Ground-glass center with a ring of consolidation; should raise suspicion of <strong>mucormycosis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Galactomannan (GM)</strong>, a polysaccharide of the <em>Aspergillus</em> cell wall released during hyphal growth<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>serum GM</strong> is most useful in <strong>neutropenic</strong> patients (angioinvasive disease) and is used for <strong>serial screening</strong> in high-risk hematology patients not on mould-active prophylaxis;</li>\n<li><strong>BAL GM is more sensitive than serum GM</strong>, especially in <strong>non-neutropenic</strong> and ICU patients;</li>\n<li><strong>mould-active prophylaxis lowers the sensitivity</strong> of serum GM.</li>\n<li><strong>β-D-glucan</strong> is a <strong>pan-fungal</strong> marker (also positive in candidiasis and <em>Pneumocystis</em> pneumonia); it is <strong>not specific for <em>Aspergillus</em></strong> and is <strong>not a mycological criterion for probable IPA</strong> in the 2020 definitions<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bronchoscopy with BAL</strong> (GM, PCR, culture, microscopy) is recommended when feasible; <strong>biopsy</strong> is needed for a proven diagnosis<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>ICU patients</strong>: the EORTC/MSGERC criteria were not designed for ICU patients without classic host factors. The <strong>AspICU algorithm</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup> and dedicated definitions for <strong>IAPA</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup> and <strong>CAPA</strong> (ECMM/ISHAM)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup> are used instead; all rely heavily on <strong>BAL GM and culture</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Susceptibility testing</strong> of <em>Aspergillus</em> isolates (EUCAST method) is recommended in invasive disease, because of azole resistance<sup class=\"cite\">[2,26]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<p><strong>When to suspect:</strong> fever or sepsis <strong>not responding to antibiotics</strong> in a patient with a <strong>CVC, TPN, recent abdominal surgery, broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> or <strong>multifocal <em>Candida</em> colonization</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,6,7]</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Strengths</th>\n<th>Limitations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Blood cultures</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Gold standard</strong>; <em>Candida</em> in a blood culture = <strong>proven candidemia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>; gives the isolate for <strong>species ID and susceptibility</strong></td>\n<td>Sensitivity for <strong>all IC only about 50%</strong> (deep-seated infection is often culture-negative); results take <strong>days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[22]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>β-D-glucan (BDG)</strong></td>\n<td>Fast; <strong>high negative predictive value</strong>; two consecutive positive results support <strong>probable IC</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></td>\n<td><strong>Not specific</strong> (any fungus except Mucorales and, mostly, <em>Cryptococcus</em>); <strong>false positives</strong> with <strong>hemodialysis</strong>, <strong>albumin/IVIG</strong>, surgical gauze, bacteremia<sup class=\"cite\">[4,7]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T2Candida</strong> (T2 magnetic resonance)</td>\n<td>Detects the 5 commonest species <strong>directly in blood in a few hours</strong>; high sensitivity in the pivotal study<sup class=\"cite\">[23]</sup></td>\n<td>Limited panel; no susceptibility result; cost and availability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mannan antigen / anti-mannan antibody</strong></td>\n<td>Combined use increases sensitivity<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup></td>\n<td>Lower performance than BDG in many settings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Culture/histology of deep sites</strong> (peritoneal fluid from surgery, abscess drainage, biopsy)</td>\n<td>Confirms deep-seated IC<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n<td><em>Candida</em> from <strong>drains</strong> or <strong>respiratory samples</strong> usually reflects colonization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Species identification</strong> (MALDI-TOF) and <strong>antifungal susceptibility testing</strong> (EUCAST or CLSI) are recommended for <strong>bloodstream and other clinically relevant isolates</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>EUCAST breakpoints (concept)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[26]</sup>: isolates are reported as <strong>S</strong> (susceptible, standard dosing), <strong>I</strong> (<strong>susceptible, increased exposure</strong>: the drug works if higher exposure is achieved) or <strong>R</strong> (resistant). For example, <strong>wild-type <em>C. glabrata</em></strong> is categorized as <strong>I</strong> to fluconazole, and <strong><em>C. krusei</em></strong> is considered <strong>intrinsically resistant</strong> to fluconazole.</li>\n<li><strong>Work-up of every candidemia</strong> (section 7.2): repeat blood cultures, <strong>dilated fundoscopy</strong>, search for the source (CVC, abdomen), and <strong>echocardiography</strong> when endocarditis is possible<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "6", "title": "Therapy", "html": "<p><em>Principles only; no dosing. Always adjust to local epidemiology and susceptibility, and seek infectious disease advice.</em></p>", "subs": [{"id": "6.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<p><strong>Antifungal classes (general)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Agents</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mould-active triazoles</strong> (inhibit ergosterol synthesis, CYP51)</td>\n<td><strong>Voriconazole</strong>, <strong>isavuconazole</strong>, <strong>posaconazole</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Many drug interactions (CYP3A4)</strong>; voriconazole: visual disturbances, hallucinations, hepatotoxicity, photosensitivity and skin cancer, periostitis, QT prolongation; isavuconazole <strong>shortens</strong> the QT interval and is better tolerated<sup class=\"cite\">[1,10,11]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polyenes</strong> (bind ergosterol)</td>\n<td><strong>Liposomal amphotericin B</strong></td>\n<td>Broad spectrum (<strong>also Mucorales</strong>); <strong>nephrotoxicity</strong>, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, infusion reactions; <em>A. terreus</em> is resistant<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Echinocandins</strong> (inhibit β-1,3-D-glucan synthase)</td>\n<td>Caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin</td>\n<td><strong>Not first-line monotherapy</strong> for IPA; used for <strong>salvage</strong> or in <strong>combination</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fluconazole</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>No activity against <em>Aspergillus</em></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>First-line treatment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voriconazole</strong> became the standard after it proved <strong>superior to amphotericin B deoxycholate</strong> in response and survival<sup class=\"cite\">[1,9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Isavuconazole</strong> was <strong>non-inferior to voriconazole</strong> with <strong>fewer drug-related adverse events</strong> (especially hepatobiliary, skin and eye)<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Posaconazole</strong> was <strong>non-inferior to voriconazole</strong> for all-cause mortality at day 42, with fewer treatment-related adverse events<sup class=\"cite\">[11]</sup>.</li>\n<li>So, current guidelines list <strong>voriconazole or isavuconazole</strong> (and now posaconazole) as <strong>first-line</strong>, and <strong>liposomal amphotericin B</strong> as the <strong>alternative</strong> (e.g. intolerance, interactions, suspected mucormycosis, azole resistance)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,11,27]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Combination therapy</strong> (voriconazole + an echinocandin): the randomized trial showed a <strong>non-significant trend</strong> toward lower mortality<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>. Guidelines allow it in <strong>selected patients</strong> (severe disease, suspected azole resistance); a 2025 ATS guideline considers <strong>either combination or triazole monotherapy</strong> acceptable<sup class=\"cite\">[1,8,12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Azole resistance:</strong> where local resistance is high (<strong>≥10%</strong>), expert guidance suggests starting with <strong>voriconazole + echinocandin</strong> or <strong>liposomal amphotericin B</strong> until susceptibility is known<sup class=\"cite\">[2,20]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adjunctive measures</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)</strong> of <strong>voriconazole</strong> (non-linear, highly variable pharmacokinetics, CYP2C19 polymorphisms) and <strong>posaconazole</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduce immunosuppression</strong> when possible (taper corticosteroids); recovery from neutropenia is the strongest predictor of outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgery</strong> for lesions <strong>near great vessels or the pericardium</strong>, <strong>massive hemoptysis</strong>, invasive <strong>sinus</strong> disease or single residual lesions before further chemotherapy/HSCT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Duration:</strong> at least <strong>6–12 weeks</strong>, and in practice <strong>until clinical and radiological resolution and recovery of immune function</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "6.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<p><strong>First-line treatment of candidemia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>echinocandin</strong> (<strong>caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin</strong>) is first-line for candidemia in most patients, because of its <strong>fungicidal activity</strong>, broad species coverage, activity on <strong>biofilms</strong>, few interactions and good safety<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5,7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rezafungin</strong>, a <strong>once-weekly</strong> echinocandin, was <strong>non-inferior to caspofungin</strong> in a phase 3 trial<sup class=\"cite\">[13]</sup> and is included as an option in recent guidance<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fluconazole</strong> is an acceptable initial choice only for <strong>non-critically ill</strong> patients with <strong>fluconazole-susceptible species</strong> (no recent azole exposure)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Liposomal amphotericin B</strong> is the alternative for <strong>intolerance, unavailability or resistance</strong> to other agents<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step-down and duration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Step down to fluconazole</strong> (oral) after <strong>5–7 days</strong> if the patient is <strong>clinically stable</strong>, the isolate is <strong>fluconazole-susceptible</strong> and <strong>repeat blood cultures are negative</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration for uncomplicated candidemia: 14 days after the first negative blood culture</strong> and resolution of symptoms<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Source control</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Remove the CVC early</strong> when it is the presumed source and can be removed safely (strong recommendation in <strong>non-neutropenic</strong> patients; decide case by case in neutropenic patients, in whom the gut is often the source)<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Drain</strong> intra-abdominal collections and repair leaks<sup class=\"cite\">[4,7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Situations that change the choice</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. glabrata</em></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Echinocandin</strong>; step down only to high-dose fluconazole or voriconazole <strong>if susceptible</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. krusei</em></strong></td>\n<td>Echinocandin, liposomal amphotericin B or voriconazole (<strong>never fluconazole</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. parapsilosis</em></strong></td>\n<td>Fluconazole may be preferred if the isolate is susceptible (higher echinocandin MICs)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>C. auris</em></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Echinocandin</strong> first-line; treat according to susceptibility; <strong>contact precautions</strong> and notification<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Endophthalmitis / chorioretinitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Echinocandins penetrate the eye poorly</strong>: use <strong>fluconazole or voriconazole</strong> (or liposomal amphotericin B ± flucytosine); <strong>intravitreal</strong> therapy and/or <strong>vitrectomy</strong> if the vitreous is involved; treat for <strong>at least 4–6 weeks</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Endocarditis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Valve surgery</strong> plus prolonged antifungal therapy, often followed by long-term fluconazole suppression<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Symptomatic cystitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fluconazole</strong> (echinocandins do not reach urine); treat <strong>asymptomatic candiduria</strong> only in <strong>neutropenic patients</strong>, <strong>very low birth weight infants</strong> or before <strong>urologic procedures</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic disseminated candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td>Prolonged therapy (often months) <strong>until lesions resolve</strong>; chemotherapy can continue<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Empiric therapy in the ICU</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Empiric antifungal therapy can be <strong>considered</strong> in critically ill patients with risk factors and <strong>unexplained septic shock</strong>, and should be <strong>stopped after a few days</strong> if there is no response and no evidence of IC<sup class=\"cite\">[4,7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>EMPIRICUS</strong> randomized trial, empiric micafungin in non-neutropenic ICU patients with multiple risk factors <strong>did not improve fungal infection-free survival</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[30]</sup>. A 2025 ATS guideline suggests <strong>against routine prophylactic or empiric antifungals</strong> in critically ill patients without neutropenia or transplant<sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "7", "title": "Follow-up", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "7.1", "title": "IPA", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical reassessment</strong> and <strong>chest CT</strong> after about <strong>2 weeks</strong> (earlier if worsening)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not over-interpret early CT:</strong> lesions often <strong>enlarge during the first 7–10 days</strong> of effective therapy, especially at <strong>neutrophil recovery</strong>; this alone is <strong>not treatment failure</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serial serum galactomannan:</strong> falling values correlate with response and survival; persistently rising values suggest failure<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>TDM</strong> for voriconazole and posaconazole, plus monitoring of <strong>liver function</strong>, <strong>QT interval</strong>, vision and drug interactions<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>If failure:</strong> check drug levels, look for <strong>resistance</strong> (repeat sampling, susceptibility testing) or <strong>another pathogen</strong> (e.g. <strong>Mucorales</strong>), then switch class (e.g. to liposomal amphotericin B)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary prophylaxis</strong> with a mould-active agent is recommended when a patient with prior IPA undergoes <strong>further immunosuppression</strong> (chemotherapy, HSCT)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.2", "title": "Invasive candidiasis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Repeat blood cultures</strong> every day or every other day <strong>until negative</strong>; the first negative culture defines the start of the 14-day course<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Persistent candidemia</strong> (positive cultures after several days of therapy) → search for a <strong>retained catheter, abscess, septic thrombophlebitis or endocarditis</strong>, and check susceptibility<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dilated fundoscopy</strong> by an ophthalmologist, ideally <strong>within the first week</strong> in non-neutropenic patients; in neutropenic patients, <strong>after neutrophil recovery</strong> (lesions may not be visible during neutropenia)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Echocardiography</strong> when endocarditis is possible (persistent candidemia, prosthetic valves, new murmur)<sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>De-escalate</strong> (step-down to oral fluconazole) and <strong>stop empiric therapy</strong> when IC is excluded: part of <strong>antifungal stewardship</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,5,7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "8", "title": "Vaccines and Prophylaxis", "html": "<p>There is <strong>no licensed vaccine</strong> against <em>Aspergillus</em> or <em>Candida</em>. Prevention relies on <strong>antifungal prophylaxis in selected high-risk patients</strong> and <strong>infection control</strong>.</p>", "subs": [{"id": "8.1", "title": "Antifungal prophylaxis", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Population</th>\n<th>Prophylaxis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AML/MDS during induction chemotherapy</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Posaconazole</strong>: in a randomized trial it <strong>reduced proven/probable IFI and IPA</strong> and <strong>improved survival</strong> compared with fluconazole/itraconazole<sup class=\"cite\">[14,16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Allogeneic HSCT with GVHD</strong> on intensive immunosuppression</td>\n<td><strong>Posaconazole</strong> (fewer invasive aspergillosis cases than fluconazole)<sup class=\"cite\">[15,16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Allogeneic HSCT, pre-engraftment</strong> (low mould risk)</td>\n<td><strong>Fluconazole</strong> (prevents candidiasis; <strong>no anti-mould activity</strong>); mould-active azole if mould risk is high<sup class=\"cite\">[1,16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lung transplant</strong></td>\n<td>Mould-active prophylaxis (e.g. <strong>inhaled amphotericin B</strong> or a triazole)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High-risk liver, pancreas, small bowel transplant</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fluconazole</strong> (or other agents according to risk)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ICU</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Not routine</strong>; fluconazole only in <strong>units with a high incidence</strong> of IC and in <strong>selected high-risk patients</strong> (e.g. <strong>recurrent gastrointestinal perforation or anastomotic leak</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[4,7,8]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neonates (very low birth weight)</strong> in nurseries with <strong>high rates</strong> of IC</td>\n<td><strong>Fluconazole</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mould-active azoles under prophylaxis</strong> need <strong>TDM</strong> (posaconazole, voriconazole) and attention to <strong>drug interactions</strong> (e.g. vincristine, venetoclax, calcineurin inhibitors)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,16]</sup>.</li>\n<li>A breakthrough infection on mould-active prophylaxis should raise suspicion of <strong>resistant moulds</strong> (e.g. Mucorales)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.2", "title": "Infection control", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Aspergillus</em></strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>: <strong>HEPA-filtered</strong>, positive-pressure rooms for allogeneic HSCT recipients; protection during <strong>hospital construction</strong>; patients should avoid gardening, compost and dusty environments.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Candida</em></strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4,5]</sup>: <strong>CVC insertion and maintenance bundles</strong>, <strong>hand hygiene</strong>, early removal of unnecessary catheters, antibiotic stewardship.</li>\n<li><strong><em>C. auris</em></strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>: <strong>screening</strong> of contacts and high-risk admissions, <strong>contact precautions</strong> (single room), <strong>thorough environmental cleaning</strong> and disinfection, and notification to public health authorities.</li>\n</ul>"}]}], "open": ["Describe the microscopic features of <em>Aspergillus</em> in tissue and explain how they differ from those of Mucorales. Why does this difference matter for therapy?", "Explain the role of alveolar macrophages and neutrophils in host defense against <em>Aspergillus</em>, and how neutropenia and corticosteroids produce different patterns of IPA.", "List the main host factors for IPA according to current consensus definitions, and name the ICU populations at risk without classic host factors.", "Define proven, probable and possible IPA, and describe the typical CT findings and their timing.", "Discuss the strengths and limitations of galactomannan (serum and BAL) and β-D-glucan in the diagnosis of invasive fungal infections.", "Which class of antifungal drugs is the first-line treatment of IPA?", "Describe the two main routes by which <em>Candida</em> reaches the bloodstream, and list the major risk factors for invasive candidiasis in the ICU.", "Why are blood cultures insufficient to diagnose all forms of invasive candidiasis? Which non-culture tests can help?", "Describe the management of candidemia in a non-neutropenic patient with a central venous catheter, including drug choice, step-down, source control, follow-up tests and duration.", "Which patients should receive antifungal prophylaxis against moulds or yeasts, and which infection control measures prevent <em>Aspergillus</em> and <em>Candida auris</em> infections?"], "refs": "<ol>\n<li>Patterson TF, Thompson GR 3rd, Denning DW, et al. Practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of aspergillosis: 2016 update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2016;63(4):e1–e60. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw326</li>\n<li>Ullmann AJ, Aguado JM, Arikan-Akdagli S, et al. Diagnosis and management of <em>Aspergillus</em> diseases: executive summary of the 2017 ESCMID-ECMM-ERS guideline. <em>Clin Microbiol Infect</em>. 2018;24(Suppl 1):e1–e38. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmi.2018.01.002</li>\n<li>Donnelly JP, Chen SC, Kauffman CA, et al. Revision and update of the consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease from the EORTC and the Mycoses Study Group Education and Research Consortium. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2020;71(6):1367–1376. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciz1008</li>\n<li>Pappas PG, Kauffman CA, Andes DR, et al. Clinical practice guideline for the management of candidiasis: 2016 update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2016;62(4):e1–e50. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/civ933</li>\n<li>Cornely OA, Sprute R, Bassetti M, et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of candidiasis: an initiative of the ECMM in cooperation with ISHAM and ASM. <em>Lancet Infect Dis</em>. 2025;25(5):e280–e293. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(24)00749-7</li>\n<li>Kullberg BJ, Arendrup MC. Invasive candidiasis. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2015;373(15):1445–1456. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMra1315399</li>\n<li>Martin-Loeches I, Antonelli M, Cuenca-Estrella M, et al. ESICM/ESCMID task force on practical management of invasive candidiasis in critically ill patients. <em>Intensive Care Med</em>. 2019;45(6):789–805. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00134-019-05599-w</li>\n<li>Epelbaum O, Marinelli T, Haydour Q, et al. Treatment of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis and preventive and empirical therapy for invasive candidiasis in adult pulmonary and critical care patients: an official American Thoracic Society clinical practice guideline. <em>Am J Respir Crit Care Med</em>. 2025;211(1):34–53. https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.202410-2045ST</li>\n<li>Herbrecht R, Denning DW, Patterson TF, et al. Voriconazole versus amphotericin B for primary therapy of invasive aspergillosis. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2002;347(6):408–415. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa020191</li>\n<li>Maertens JA, Raad II, Marr KA, et al. Isavuconazole versus voriconazole for primary treatment of invasive mould disease caused by <em>Aspergillus</em> and other filamentous fungi (SECURE): a phase 3, randomised-controlled, non-inferiority trial. <em>Lancet</em>. 2016;387(10020):760–769. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(15)01159-9</li>\n<li>Maertens JA, Rahav G, Lee DG, et al. Posaconazole versus voriconazole for primary treatment of invasive aspergillosis: a phase 3, randomised, controlled, non-inferiority trial. <em>Lancet</em>. 2021;397(10273):499–509. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(21)00219-1</li>\n<li>Marr KA, Schlamm HT, Herbrecht R, et al. Combination antifungal therapy for invasive aspergillosis: a randomized trial. <em>Ann Intern Med</em>. 2015;162(2):81–89. https://doi.org/10.7326/M13-2508</li>\n<li>Thompson GR 3rd, Soriano A, Cornely OA, et al. Rezafungin versus caspofungin for treatment of candidaemia and invasive candidiasis (ReSTORE): a multicentre, double-blind, double-dummy, randomised phase 3 trial. <em>Lancet</em>. 2023;401(10370):49–59. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(22)02324-3</li>\n<li>Cornely OA, Maertens J, Winston DJ, et al. Posaconazole vs. fluconazole or itraconazole prophylaxis in patients with neutropenia. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2007;356(4):348–359. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa061094</li>\n<li>Ullmann AJ, Lipton JH, Vesole DH, et al. Posaconazole or fluconazole for prophylaxis in severe graft-versus-host disease. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2007;356(4):335–347. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa061098</li>\n<li>Maertens JA, Girmenia C, Brüggemann RJ, et al. European guidelines for primary antifungal prophylaxis in adult haematology patients: summary of the updated recommendations from the European Conference on Infections in Leukaemia (ECIL). <em>J Antimicrob Chemother</em>. 2018;73(12):3221–3230. https://doi.org/10.1093/jac/dky286</li>\n<li>Koehler P, Bassetti M, Chakrabarti A, et al. Defining and managing COVID-19-associated pulmonary aspergillosis: the 2020 ECMM/ISHAM consensus criteria for research and clinical guidance. <em>Lancet Infect Dis</em>. 2021;21(6):e149–e162. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(20)30847-1</li>\n<li>Verweij PE, Rijnders BJA, Brüggemann RJM, et al. Review of influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis in ICU patients and proposal for a case definition: an expert opinion. <em>Intensive Care Med</em>. 2020;46(8):1524–1535. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00134-020-06091-6</li>\n<li>Blot SI, Taccone FS, Van den Abeele AM, et al. A clinical algorithm to diagnose invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in critically ill patients. <em>Am J Respir Crit Care Med</em>. 2012;186(1):56–64. https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.201111-1978OC</li>\n<li>Verweij PE, Ananda-Rajah M, Andes D, et al. International expert opinion on the management of infection caused by azole-resistant <em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em>. <em>Drug Resist Updat</em>. 2015;21–22:30–40. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.drup.2015.08.001</li>\n<li>León C, Ruiz-Santana S, Saavedra P, et al. A bedside scoring system (\"Candida score\") for early antifungal treatment in nonneutropenic critically ill patients with <em>Candida</em> colonization. <em>Crit Care Med</em>. 2006;34(3):730–737. https://doi.org/10.1097/01.CCM.0000202208.37364.7D</li>\n<li>Clancy CJ, Nguyen MH. Finding the \"missing 50%\" of invasive candidiasis: how nonculture diagnostics will improve understanding of disease spectrum and transform patient care. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2013;56(9):1284–1292. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/cit006</li>\n<li>Mylonakis E, Clancy CJ, Ostrosky-Zeichner L, et al. T2 magnetic resonance assay for the rapid diagnosis of candidemia in whole blood: a clinical trial. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2015;60(6):892–899. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciu959</li>\n<li>Lamoth F, Lockhart SR, Berkow EL, Calandra T. Changes in the epidemiological landscape of invasive candidiasis. <em>J Antimicrob Chemother</em>. 2018;73(Suppl 1):i4–i13. https://doi.org/10.1093/jac/dkx444</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. WHO fungal priority pathogens list to guide research, development and public health action. Geneva: WHO; 2022. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240060241</li>\n<li>European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (EUCAST). Clinical breakpoint tables for antifungal agents (current version). https://www.eucast.org/astoffungi/clinicalbreakpointsforantifungals/ (accessed November 2026)</li>\n<li>Wichmann D, Hoenigl M, Koehler P, et al. Diagnosis and treatment of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in critically ill intensive care patients: executive summary of the German national guideline (AWMF 113-005). <em>Infection</em>. 2025;53(4):1299–1310. https://doi.org/10.1007/s15010-025-02572-2</li>\n<li>Bennett JE, Dolin R, Blaser MJ, eds. <em>Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases</em>. 9th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2020 (chapters on <em>Aspergillus</em> species and <em>Candida</em> species).</li>\n<li>Wisplinghoff H, Bischoff T, Tallent SM, et al. Nosocomial bloodstream infections in US hospitals: analysis of 24,179 cases from a prospective nationwide surveillance study. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2004;39(3):309–317. https://doi.org/10.1086/421946</li>\n<li>Timsit JF, Azoulay E, Schwebel C, et al. Empirical micafungin treatment and survival without invasive fungal infection in adults with ICU-acquired sepsis, <em>Candida</em> colonization, and multiple organ failure: the EMPIRICUS randomized clinical trial. <em>JAMA</em>. 2016;316(15):1555–1564. https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.14655</li>\n</ol>"};
